        "workedSolution": "(i) Identification of parts:\n• Part I: **Brass cap (conducting disc)**\n• Part II: **Insulating plug (rubber or ebonite stopper)**\n• Part III: **Brass rod (conducting stem)**\n• Part IV: **Metal case with glass window (earthed enclosure)**\n• Part V: **Gold leaf (thin flexible metal foil)**\n\n(ii) Functions of parts:\n• Part II (Insulating plug): Prevents electrostatic charges on the cap and rod from leaking to the outer metal casing.\n• Part IV (Metal case): Shields the delicate gold leaf from external air currents and electrostatic interference while allowing visual observation through glass.\n\n(iii) Observation on gold leaf V:\nThe gold leaf V **diverges (moves away from the fixed brass plate)**.\n\n(iv) Scientific explanation:\nWhen a negatively charged rod approaches cap I, negative electrons in the cap are repelled downwards along conducting rod III to the brass plate and gold leaf V. Because both the leaf and plate acquire like negative charges, they repel each other, causing divergence.\n\n(v) Practical applications of an electroscope:\n1. Detecting the presence of electrostatic electric charges on bodies.\n2. Determining the polarity (nature) of an unknown electric charge (positive or negative).\n3. Testing whether a material is an electrical conductor or an electrical insulator.",
