        "workedSolution": "(i) Anatomical representations:\n• Part I (Glass tube): **Trachea (windpipe)**\n• Part II (Y-tube): **Bronchi (left and right bronchus)**\n• Part III (Balloons): **Lungs**\n• Part IV (Bell jar): **Thoracic cavity (ribcage and chest wall)**\n• Part V (Rubber sheet): **Diaphragm**\n\n(ii) Observation when rubber sheet V is pulled downwards:\nBalloons III **expand and inflate (fill with air)**.\n\n(iii) Explanation of mechanism (Inhalation):\nPulling rubber sheet V downward increases the internal volume of bell jar IV. This volume increase causes internal air pressure inside the jar to fall below atmospheric pressure. Outside air rushes down tube I and Y-tube II into balloons III, inflating them until pressure equalizes.\n\n(iv) Limitations of the bell-jar model:\n1. The bell jar has rigid glass walls, whereas the human ribcage expands upward and outward via intercostal muscles.\n2. The flat rubber sheet moves only up and down, whereas the human diaphragm is dome-shaped when relaxed.",
