        "workedSolution": "(i) Identification of soil samples:\n• Tube A (lowest final rise): **Sandy soil**\n• Tube B (intermediate rise): **Loamy soil**\n• Tube C (highest final rise): **Clayey soil**\n\n(ii) Capillary performance:\n• (α) Greatest capillary rise: **Soil C (Clayey soil)**\n• (β) Fastest initial rate: **Soil A (Sandy soil)** — large macropores allow rapid initial water entry, but low surface tension forces limit total lift.\n\n(iii) Explanation of highest rise in Soil C:\nClayey soil consists of fine mineral particles (< 0.002 mm) that create microscopic, continuous capillary micropores. High adhesive forces between water molecules and clay particles pull water upward against gravity to a greater height than the wide macropores of sandy soil.\n\n(iv) Agricultural disadvantages of Soil C:\n1. **Waterlogging and Poor Aeration:** Fine pores drain water poorly, displacing soil oxygen and suffocating plant roots.\n2. **Difficult Tillage:** Clay becomes sticky and heavy when wet and hardens into bricks when dry, making mechanized ploughing difficult.",
