        "prompt": "(i) State two physical differences between diamond and graphite.\n(ii) Explain why graphite is capable of conducting electricity whereas diamond is an electrical insulator.",
        "workedSolution": "(i) Diamond vs. Graphite differences:\n1. **Hardness:** Diamond is the hardest known natural mineral due to its rigid 3D tetrahedral network; graphite is soft and slippery because its hexagonal layers slide over one another.\n2. **Appearance:** Diamond is transparent and brilliant with a high refractive index; graphite is opaque, black, and metallic-gray.\n\n(ii) Electrical conductivity explanation:\nIn diamond, each carbon atom uses all four valence electrons in strong covalent bonds with four neighboring carbon atoms in a tetrahedral lattice, leaving no free electrons. In graphite, each carbon atom bonds with only three neighbors in planar sheets, leaving one delocalized electron per carbon atom free to move and conduct current through the crystal.",
